    "lawCode": "Amd §2808, rpld §2808 sub 2-c ¶(f), Pub Health L",
    "lawSection": "Public Health Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S6559\n\n TITLE OF BILL :  An act to amend the public health law, in relation\nto payment for reserved bed days in residential health care\nfacilities; and to repeal paragraph (f) of subdivision 2-c of section\n2808 of such law relating to prospective per diem adjustments for\ncertain nursing homes\n\n PURPOSE :\n\nTo remedy actions proposed by the Executive and enacted in the FY 2018\nbudget, which eliminated the State's Medicaid reserve bed day\nreimbursement methodology and protections that ensured certain nursing\nhome patients who must go to the hospital for up to 14 days in a\ntwelve month period do not lose their nursing home bed.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nSection 1 of the bill amends chapter 57 of the laws of 2017 to restore\nlong standing provisions of law which provide that nursing homes shall\nbe paid for \"reserve bed days\" and hold a residents bed if they are\nhospitalized for up to 14 days in a given year.\n\nSection 2 repeals paragraph (f) of subdivision 2-c of section 2808\nwhich provides for a $18 million dollar cut associated with the\nelimination of reserve bed day reimbursement.\n\nSection 3 provides an effective date\n\n EXISTING LAW :\n\nCurrently section 2808 of the public health law only provides\nreimbursement for reserve bed days while a resident is on therapeutic\nleave of absence from the facility. As a result, if a resident must be\nadmitted to the hospital, they risk the loss of their nursing home bed\nuntil another one becomes available and may be forced to spend more\ntime than necessary in the hospital.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nNursing home resident's often spend many years in the same facility,\nroom and bed and many spend down their resources and end up qualifying\nfor Medicaid. The nursing home is their home. New York State has had a\nlong standing policy which provides nursing homes with high occupancy\nrates a reduced Medicaid payment to hold a resident's same bed if they\nend up having to be admitted to the hospital. The rate is a percentage\nof the full rate and is only reimbursed for up to 14 days in any\ncalendar year for a particular resident. This policy has ensured that\nresidents can come home to the same facility and bed. It reduces\nenormous disruption to residents who have come to feel comfortable in\ntheir particular room and with a possible roommate. It has also\nensured that nursing home residents are not needlessly waiting in the\nhospital until a bed becomes available at a much higher cost to the\nhealth care system. Delayed hospital discharges cause a ripple effect\nthroughout the health care system.\n\nSince April 1, 2017 when reserve bed day payments were to cease, there\nhas been considerable confusion and disruption. As a result, in May\n2017 the Department of Health issued guidance to nursing home\nadministrators indicating that they would be temporarily continuing\nreserve bed day payments as they existed before April 1st. The\nDepartment of Health's directive also indicated it plans \"to make\nconforming changes to its regulations, on an emergency basis, making\nclear that Medicaid reimbursement for reserve bed days for temporarily\nhospitalized patients under 21 is still available and that nursing\nhomes continue to have an obligation to reserve the same bed for a\ntemporarily hospitalized patient who is 21 years of age or older for\n14 days, regardless of the availability of Medicaid reserve bed day\nreimbursement.\"\n\nWhile the Department indicates these actions are to clarify the\nmeaning and intent of the budget actions, it was not in fact the\nintent or understanding that by eliminating the reserve bed day\npayments completely, nursing homes would continue to be responsible\nfor holding the residents same bed. This legislation restores the\nstatute and reserve bed day payments as they existed prior to April 1,\n2017 to ensure that nursing homes, nursing home residents and the\nhospitals and health care system are not adversely affected.\n\n LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\nNew bill\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\n\nTo be determined, however the Department of Health has issued guidance\ndelaying implementation and continuing reserve bed day payments\npending the adoption of emergency regulations in order to \"clarify the\nmeaning and intent of the statute\".\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nImmediately and shall be deemed to have been in full force and effect\non and after April 1, 2017.\n",
